.TH "UBERTOOTH\-FOLLOW" "1" "October 2026" "Project Ubertooth" "User Commands"
.SH "NAME"
\fBubertooth\-follow\fR \- active (BlueZ) CLK discovery and follow for a target address
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.nf
ubertooth\-follow [\-t BD_ADDR] [\-l LAP] [\-u UAP] [\-U <index|serial>]
[\-r file] [\-q file] [\-d file] [\-e errors] [\-a] [\-b hciN] [\-w delay]
.fi
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
Follows a classic Bluetooth (BR/EDR) piconet for a given target address\. It uses a local BlueZ Bluetooth device to assist with clock discovery and an Ubertooth to follow the piconet's frequency hopping\.
.P
Name the target one of two ways:
.IP "\(bu" 4
\fB\-t\fR is its whole address, \fBxx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx\fR, in either case of hex digit\. That is what the tool connects to, and it gives the LAP and UAP the board follows, so \fB\-t\fR needs neither \fB\-l\fR nor \fB\-u\fR\. Use \fB\-t\fR for a real target: it is the only form \fB\-a\fR works with, and the only one that reaches an address not beginning \fB00:00\fR (see AFH below)\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
\fB\-l\fR and \fB\-u\fR together are the lower and upper address parts, which is what \fBubertooth\-rx\fR reports for a piconet it has seen\. They are four of the six bytes of an address; the two missing ones are the non\-significant address part, which a sniffer never sees, and the tool fills them in as zero\. So the connection it makes to collect the clock offset only reaches a target whose address begins \fB00:00\fR\.
.IP "" 0
.P
One of \fB\-l\fR and \fB\-u\fR without the other names no piconet, and the tool does not refuse it today: it reads the address from the local Bluetooth device as if no target had been given, and follows that instead of what was asked for\.
.P
\fB\-t\fR with \fB\-l\fR or \fB\-u\fR is refused: the two are the same setting written two ways, and the tool does not reconcile them\. With no target at all, the address is read from the local Bluetooth device \(em note this may yield the wrong address in some cases, so naming the target explicitly is recommended\.
.P
The capture is gated by the board's squelch, which this tool does not set: it is the default \-120 dBm unless \fBubertooth\-util \-z\fR set another value while the board was idle, and the firmware resets it on every mode change, so it never carries over from an earlier capture\. See ubertooth\-util(1), SQUELCH\.
.P
Requires BlueZ (Linux only)\.
.SH "OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-t <BD_ADDR>\fR
the target's whole address, \fBxx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx\fR, which gives the LAP and UAP to follow as well as the address to connect to\. Not with \fB\-l\fR or \fB\-u\fR, and given once\.
.TP
\fB\-l <LAP>\fR
target LAP (3 bytes / 6 hex digits), given once\. Not with \fB\-t\fR\.
.TP
\fB\-u <UAP>\fR
target UAP (1 byte / 2 hex digits), given once\. Not with \fB\-t\fR\.
.TP
\fB\-U <index|serial>\fR
select an Ubertooth by enumeration index or serial number; see ubertooth(7), SELECTING A DEVICE
.TP
\fB\-r <filename>\fR
capture packets to a PCAPNG file\.
.TP
\fB\-q <filename>\fR
capture packets to a PCAP file\.
.TP
\fB\-d <filename>\fR
dump received symbols to the named file\.
.TP
\fB\-e <0\-5>\fR
maximum access\-code errors to tolerate (default: 2)\.
.TP
\fB\-a\fR
enable AFH (adaptive frequency hopping); experimental, and needs \fB\-t\fR\. See AFH below\.
.TP
\fB\-b <hciN>\fR
local Bluetooth device to use (default: hci0)\.
.TP
\fB\-w <timeslots>\fR
USB delay in 625us timeslots (default: 5)\.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR
show help\.
.SH "AFH"
\fB\-a\fR is experimental\. It makes the local BlueZ device connect to the target and asks the local controller for the AFH channel map of \fIthat\fR link, then follows the piconet keyed on the target's LAP/UAP\. The two are the same hop sequence only when the target is master of the piconet you want to follow, which \fBubertooth\-follow\fR cannot check\. Treat a capture made with \fB\-a\fR accordingly\.
.P
Because the map is read over a connection to the target, \fB\-a\fR needs the whole address, which only \fB\-t\fR gives\. \fB\-a\fR without \fB\-t\fR is refused before any file is created or device opened, with \fBerror: \-a reads the AFH map over a Bluetooth connection to the target, so it needs the whole target address: give \-t xx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx\fR\.
.P
A follow keyed on \fB\-l\fR and \fB\-u\fR instead connects to \fB00:00:<UAP>:<LAP>\fR, with the two most significant bytes zero, because a sniffer cannot see them\. That reaches only a target whose address begins \fB00:00\fR; for any other target the connection fails and the command stops with \fBerror: connecting to 00:00:\|\.\|\.\|\.: failed\fR\. Give the target as \fB\-t\fR and the connection is made to the address as it is\.
.P
The map that comes back is used only when it could be a hop sequence at all:
.IP "\(bu" 4
a failed read stops the command (\fBerror: AFH map read failed: \|\.\|\.\|\.\fR), rather than following with whatever the map happened to contain\. \fB\-a\fR therefore does not work on a controller that does not implement HCI_Read_AFH_Channel_Map; run without \fB\-a\fR on one of those;
.IP "\(bu" 4
a reply of AFH off is followed without it: \fBthe remote device is not using AFH; following without it\fR\. Nothing is sent to the board in that case: starting the follow resets its hop mode and AFH map, so a map left by an earlier run cannot make it hop adaptively anyway\. An accepted map is applied once the follow has started, for the same reason (see ubertooth\-util(1), CARRY\-OVER BETWEEN RUNS);
.IP "\(bu" 4
a map with fewer than the 20 channels AFH requires, or none at all, is read again \(em three reads in total, 200ms apart \(em in case it has not settled\. If it still reports too few, the command stops and says so;
.IP "\(bu" 4
a mode byte that is neither off nor on is refused and printed\.
.IP "" 0
.P
Nothing is set on the Ubertooth until the map has been accepted, so a refused \fB\-a\fR leaves the board alone, and the connection the tool made to the target is dropped on the way out\.
.SH "EXAMPLES"
Follow a target whose address you know, writing a PCAP:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-follow \-t 11:22:33:44:55:66 \-q capture\.pcap
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Follow the same target with an AFH map (see AFH above):
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-follow \-t 11:22:33:44:55:66 \-a
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Follow a piconet by the LAP and UAP \fBubertooth\-rx\fR reported for it\. This connects to \fB00:00:33:44:55:66\fR, so it works only against a target whose address really does begin \fB00:00\fR; against any other it stops with \fBerror: connecting to 00:00:33:44:55:66 failed\fR\. Use \fB\-t\fR for a real target:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-follow \-l 445566 \-u 33 \-q capture\.pcap
.fi
.IP "" 0
.SH "ARGUMENTS"
\fBubertooth\-follow\fR takes options only\. A word that is not an option, including a bare \fB\-\fR, is refused before any device is opened and named: \fBerror: unexpected argument '<word>'\fR, with exit status 1\.
.P
\fB\-h\fR ends the command only once the whole command line has parsed, so a stray word or a bad option value is reported even alongside it\.
.P
An option the tool does not know is refused the same way, as one line on standard error with no usage text after it: \fBerror: unrecognized option '\-Z'\fR\. An option whose value is missing reads \fBerror: option '\-l' requires a value\fR\.
.P
An option value must be a whole value in its range, or it is refused the same way instead of being read as 0, for example \fBerror: LAP must be 6 hex digits (got 4466)\fR and \fBerror: target address must be xx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx (got 11:22:33:44:55)\fR\.
.P
Two more kinds of command line are refused before any device is opened, each with its one line:
.IP "\(bu" 4
\fB\-t\fR, \fB\-l\fR or \fB\-u\fR given twice, \fBerror: give \-t once\fR, \fBerror: give \-l once\fR, \fBerror: give \-u once\fR: the tool does not pick between two answers to the same question;
.IP "\(bu" 4
\fB\-t\fR with \fB\-l\fR or \fB\-u\fR, \fBerror: \-t gives the whole target address, so it cannot be combined with \-l or \-u\fR\.
.IP "" 0
.P
What the tool is following \(em the target address, or the LAP and UAP it read from the local device \(em is reported on standard error, with the rest of the tool's progress\. Standard output carries the decoded packets, and, with \fB\-a\fR, the accepted channel map, which libbtbb prints itself\.
.P
A refused command line creates and truncates nothing: \fB\-r\fR, \fB\-q\fR and \fB\-d\fR name their file while the command line is read, and it is opened only once every check has passed\.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
A follow that was interrupted (Ctrl\-C) exits 0 with whatever it captured, and so does \fB\-h\fR\. A refused command line exits 1 (see ARGUMENTS), as does a file that cannot be opened and a target the tool could not set up a follow on\.
.P
A board that refuses one of the requests a follow is built on \(em the target's address, the clock reset, or the AFH map \(em exits 1 before the capture starts, with the request named on standard error\. Following anyway would mean a board that is not set up for the piconet: without the target's address it matches no packet, and with its clock never zeroed it hops on a clock the offset was not measured from\.
.P
A follow whose USB stream fails part\-way also exits 1: the board stopped answering, or dropped off the bus, so the capture stops there and holds only what arrived before it\. The failure is reported on standard error as it happens, as \fBerror: receive transfer failed: <reason>\fR\. A board that never hands the transfer back at all exits 1 too, reported by the \fBwarning:\fR line ubertooth(7) describes rather than that error\.
